## Transcript

[00:00:01] In Antarctica, you're never allowed to pee on the ice, not even when you're hiking for hours. It's because it's so cold and so dry there that when it snows, it basically never melts. So, it just stacks up into layers and layers of ice and traps air inside. And then scientists drill ice cores to study that air going back hundreds of thousands of years to learn what the environment was like back then. But, if anything else hits that ice like ash from volcanic eruptions, that gets trapped, too. So, you can't pee because if you did, one day some
[00:00:29] day some >> descendant of yours would be taking an ice core in Antarctica, and they'd dig and dig and dig, and they'd pull it out, and they'd say, "What's that yellow line?"
